{-
   What is not an origin label - the rewritten stage's copy.

   # resolver: ownership

   The **same claims in the same words** as `resolve/Reject.OriginLabel.yana`. The rule belongs to
   the *lexer* - `Token::OriginLabel` fuses a name and the marker only when the name is unqualified,
   lowercase and written against it - so both stages inherit it and both should be seen to.

   A shared rule asserted once is a rule that can quietly stop holding in the stage the fixture does
   not name, which is what the pair of files is for.

   **One line more than the shipping stage's golden**, and it is the difference worth having a pair
   of files to see: this stage reports the bare `yield` and then reports the type form it could not
   read, where the shipping one stops at the first. A cascade rather than a second claim - both
   lines are about the same written type - and it is recorded rather than filtered because a golden
   that hid it would be asserting an output nothing produces.
-}
-- What is not an origin label, now that `src'` is one token - `Token::OriginLabel`.
--
-- The lexer fuses a name and the marker only when the name is unqualified, lowercase and written
-- directly against it. That is not a restriction added on top: it is what a label *is*, and having
-- the lexer say so is what let the type parser stop deciding it. `parseLoanLabel` had been saving
-- and restoring the lexer to find out, and the branch reading `v'a` was copied into `parseType` and
-- `parseAType` because both had to make the same decision from the same two tokens.

data Cell {v: Int}

-- A space. The marker is part of the name rather than a token that happens to follow it.
fn spaced(c: src 'Cell) -> Int = c.v

-- Capitalized. A group is named the way a type variable is, and for the same reason: it is bound by
-- the declaration rather than declared somewhere a reader can look it up.
fn capitalized(c: Src'Cell) -> Int = c.v

-- Qualified, and this is the one that used to be *accepted*: `Core.thing'Cell` resolved as a group
-- named `Core.thing`. A group is numbered per declaration, so there is no name outside one for it to
-- be - which makes a qualified label a group nothing can be in.
fn qualified(c: Core.thing'Cell) -> Int = c.v

-- And `yield` with no marker, which is the other half of the same rule. `yield'` is a hard keyword
-- rather than a name the lexer fuses, precisely so that no program can write a label spelled that
-- way - so a bare `yield` in type position is the keyword written where a type belongs, and what a
-- reader who forgot the marker is told is about the marker rather than about an unknown name.
fn bare(n: yield) -> Int = 0

fn main() -> Int = 0
